finding: 1. MaterialAssets gains an ungrounded second ownership model. material-type.ts adds exclusive component/asset modes, mode-specific refusal, alpha lookup and publication; component-material.ts adds a generic component material facility solely for Sprite. Bevy's bevy_pbr Material is an Asset (material.rs:147), whereas bevy_sprite_render owns SpriteMeta buffers and its pipeline. The Render bevy entry grounds neither component-owned MaterialAssets nor this generalization. Bulk upload requires a bound GpuTable, not component ownership inside the asset allocator. Least design: Sprite owns its bound parameter table and GPU publication, with only the narrow standard material-runtime/table-source seam needed to use that table, row alpha and pipeline keys. Keep SpriteMaterialType on the standard mesh/shadow path and shared transparent phase; do not copy Bevy's separate sprite pipeline. Leave MaterialAssets asset-only. This retains the settled rendering structure without imposing Sprite's lifetime/refusals on every asset material. 2. Dropping the CPU cullBounds mirror changes transparent ordering and culling, not merely where bounds live. SpriteSystem now creates MeshInstance with default bounds. queueTransparent (forward.ts:850-892) falls back to the raw unit quad's mesh bounds, centered at (0.5,0.5,0), instead of the old origin-centered sphere, and uses that transformed center as its sorting position. Rotation about Y moves that center in depth; a sprite at z=0 rotated +90 degrees about Y now sorts at z=-0.5 instead of z=0 and can reverse order against an overlapping transparent item at z=-0.25. The gpuCullBounds branch also bypasses CPU frustum rejection entirely: offscreen Blend sprites now enter the phase and CPU-built indirect instance counts (forward.ts:1024), and can split compatible runs. The changed culling tests cover GPU Mask bounds, not transparent order, offscreen phase membership or draw counts. Smallest fix: provide the CPU transparent consumer the correct origin-centered sprite sort point and derive its conservative sphere directly from bound source columns when queuing (no retained mirror or per-row field writes), and add overlapping rotated transparent and off-frustum Blend regressions. Keep GPU bounds publication for opaque/shadow work. Independently read: full candidate diff; unit premise/grounding/stage 7; AGENTS/CONTRIBUTING; Render bevy entry; Bevy sprite alpha/record, sprite renderer registration and owned buffers, PBR Material asset contract; standard transparent queue/indirect construction; sprite membership/disposal and bound table lifetime. Ran alpha, bulk, culling, component-material, compile, floor, shadows and shared-anchor subjects: 23 tests passed. Counted 0 setAt / 0 mesh-field setters; Sprite compile count 28; floor independently reports 7/7 sprite storage and 3 compute storage, on Apple M4 Max Metal. Extra compute pipeline has an actual publication job, but that does not justify its ownership abstraction. Notes/uncertainty: Mask(threshold)/Blend names and restricted values match the allocated stage; default Mask(0.5) and Unity-derived fill renames preserve authored semantics. Migration replacements are accurate for these fields, but omit the transparent behavioral regression above. Sharing while the owner lives, row reuse and asset refusal pass; no new disposal defect found by inspection, no independent retention run. The bulk counter catches the former implementation's setters, not arbitrary direct per-row byte writes; current source does use bound-column fill. Did not rerun frame oracle: its unrotated all-sprite fixture cannot detect the independently identified cross-renderer depth-order change. Existing byte-identical fixture evidence is not a proof against it. No source edits.
